WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.932 --> 00:00:05.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.460
SPEAKER: Hey.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.960
Today we're going to talk about
how to build a custom entity

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.490
extraction model with Google
Cloud AutoML Natural Language.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.818
If you've played with the
Natural Language API before,

00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:18.110
you might have seen that it
can extract common entities

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.470
from text, like the fact that
Google is an organization

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.000
or that iPhone is
a consumer good.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.960
But what if you want to tag
more domain-specific entities,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.450
like types of diseases or
the specialized terminology

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.570
you might find in legal
documents or patents?

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.360
For that, we'll use AutoML to
train and build a custom entity

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.280
extraction model.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.100
First we'll need a
labeled data set.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.770
Today we'll build a model that
analyzes patents from Google's

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.840
public patent data set.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.080
Using Google Vision's OCR
API, we extracted the text

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.900
from a set of PDFs of patents.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.900
Here we've store that data in a
JSON L file, which is the data

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.280
type that AutoML Natural
Language expects,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.180
and we've manually
tagged within that text

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.200
the character offsets
of the custom entities

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.110
we want to extract.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.930
AutoML Natural Language expects
to read our JSON L files

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:08.360
from a Google Cloud bucket.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.190
In addition to our
JSON L data, we'll

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.650
make a CSV file that tells
it where in Google Cloud

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.630
we've stored our data.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.370
Here we specify which
data files should

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.050
be used for
training, validation,

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.390
and testing of our model.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.790
If you don't break your data
set into these three files,

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:26.730
AutoML Natural Language will
do it for you automatically.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.790
Now let's upload our data.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.070
Here in the AutoML Natural
Language interface,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.320
we'll create a new data set.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.230
To import the data, we give
AutoML Natural Language the CSV

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.320
file we made, and then we wait.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.570
Importing data can take a
couple of minutes to finish,

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.600
but you'll get an
email and it's done.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.460
Once our data is
imported we can view

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.367
individual examples to make sure
they were labeled correctly.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.700
We can check where in the
text different custom entities

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.260
were annotated.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.060
We can also annotate
a new entity right

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.323
here from within the tool.

00:01:59.323 --> 00:02:01.490
Notice that we can also
tell how many examples we've

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:02.960
got for each label.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.090
We can begin training a
model by navigating over

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.940
to the Train tab and
clicking Start Training.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.250
Training a model can
take up to three hours.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.610
Behind the scenes, AutoML will
do a neural architecture search

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.620
to find the best model it can.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.890
So now's a good time
to take a coffee break.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.537
You'll get an email when the
model's finished training.

00:02:20.537 --> 00:02:23.120
When training is finished, we'll
be able to see our model here

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.598
in the Train tab.

00:02:24.598 --> 00:02:26.390
We can see how well it
did by clicking here

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.210
to See Full Evaluation.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.860
Here in the Evaluate
panel, we can

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.740
see the performance of our
model on different labels.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.100
The metrics we'll want to look
at are precision and recall.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.810
Precision tells
us of the snippets

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.010
the model labeled Applicant,
how many were really Applicants?

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.040
Recall tells us of all the real
Applicants in our data set,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.767
what percent were accurately
recognized by the model?

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:52.100
We can also scroll down to
see where the model correctly

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.820
labeled Applicant, these
are the true positives,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.620
and where it accidentally
mislabeled text as Applicant,

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.780
or false positives.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.180
In the Test and Use tab,
we can start actually

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.693
using our model
hosted by Google.

00:03:04.693 --> 00:03:06.860
To do that, we first have
to deploy our model, which

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.870
could take a few minutes.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.530
Next we can try to extract
custom entities on a new text

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.870
snippet.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:14.830
There we go.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.470
Here are the custom
entities we extracted.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.490
That's all for now.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.060
Have fun modeling.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

